{n:"Bacon, The Advancement of Learning", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1605; expanded in Latin 1623", era:"Early modern",
 by:"Francis Bacon", wk:"The_Advancement_of_Learning",
 mn:"mixed", es:"market", mo:"reward", sc:"graded", au:"state", f:"t",
 read:"Book I, the passage on the true end of knowledge; Book II, the address to the king on the universities and the section on final causes; <i>Novum Organum</i> I.38–44 on the idols (1620)", tr:"Original English; ed. Brian Vickers in <i>Francis Bacon: The Major Works</i> (Oxford, 2002)", ol:[["Full text (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/5500"]],
 w:"A survey of the state of knowledge addressed to James I, and the founding prospectus of modern science as an organized public enterprise. Bacon proposed a census of what was known and what was missing, and complained that the colleges of Europe were all dedicated to the professions, with none left free for the arts and sciences at large.",
 p:"A creature whose understanding is like a false mirror that distorts what it receives, through the idols of the tribe, the cave, the marketplace and the theatre. In the <i>Novum Organum</i> the Fall cost humanity both its innocence and its dominion over creation; religion can repair the first loss, and the arts and sciences the second.",
 e:"‘The glory of the Creator and the relief of man's estate’: knowledge as a rich storehouse of works for human use, not a couch for a restless mind or a tower for a proud one. The end is human use, set from outside the nature studied.",
 x:"A method rather than a body of doctrine: collect and test particulars, correct the idols, and rise slowly to general axioms. He also complained that students came too soon and too unripe to logic and rhetoric.",
 mv:"Use and charity. People seek knowledge, Bacon says, from curiosity, for ornament and reputation, to win arguments or for profit, and seldom to give a true account of their gift of reason for the benefit and use of men.",
 scp:"A college of researchers with divided labours, supported by the crown.",
 k:"Not one master but an institution. In the <i>New Atlantis</i> (1627) Salomon's House is a college of researchers with divided labours, the ancestor of the scientific academy and the research university.",
 wo:"Knowledge is for works. He wanted the trades and crafts studied and their knowledge collected as part of natural history.",
 mc:"Nothing he names. His method was meant to leave little to the sharpness of wits and place all understandings nearly on a level (<i>Novum Organum</i> I.61), which is the programme machines continue.",
 o:"Bacon removed final causes from the study of nature: in the Latin <i>De Augmentis</i> (1623) he calls their search barren, like a virgin consecrated to God, which produces nothing. Horkheimer and Adorno open <i>Dialectic of Enlightenment</i> (1944) with Bacon, arguing that knowledge pursued as power over nature ends as power over human beings. For this atlas his importance is that a nature without ends, human nature included, can receive its purpose only from outside."},

{n:"Locke, Some Thoughts Concerning Education", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1693", era:"Early modern",
 by:"John Locke", wk:"Some_Thoughts_Concerning_Education",
 mn:"neutral", es:"state", mo:"honour", sc:"restricted", au:"teacher", f:"f",
 read:"§§1, 33, 56–58, 64–66, 81, 134, 216; with <i>Essay Concerning Human Understanding</i> II.1.2", tr:"Original English; ed. John and Jean Yolton (1989)", ol:[["Full text (Standard Ebooks)","https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/john-locke/some-thoughts-concerning-education/text/single-page"]],
 w:"Letters of advice to a friend on raising his son, published as a book that dominated English and then European thinking about children for a century. Locke says that of all the people we meet, nine parts in ten are what they are, good or bad, useful or not, by their education (§1).",
 p:"The mind at birth is like white paper void of characters (<i>Essay</i> II.1.2), and the child like ‘white paper, or wax, to be moulded and fashioned as one pleases’ (§216). Reason develops; habit forms it early.",
 e:"A virtuous, prudent and useful gentleman. The great principle of all virtue is that a person can deny himself his own desires and follow what reason directs (§33).",
 x:"In order of importance: virtue, wisdom, breeding, and last learning (§134). Habits matter more than rules (§§64–66).",
 mv:"Esteem and disgrace, which Locke calls the most powerful incentives once a child can feel them, in place of beating and of rewards that feed the appetites (§56).",
 scp:"The <i>Thoughts</i> are for a gentleman's son. Locke's 1697 proposal on the poor law offered the children of the poor ‘working schools’ instead.",
 k:"A tutor of good breeding who governs by esteem and disgrace rather than beating (§§56–58), and who reasons with children as soon as they can understand (§81).",
 wo:"A gentleman should learn a manual trade, such as gardening or carpentry, for health and diversion (§§201–9).",
 mc:"Self-denial: the power to refuse one's own desire when reason directs.",
 o:"Leibniz, in the <i>New Essays</i>, replied that the mind is less like blank paper than like a block of marble with veins that mark out the shape of a figure. Rousseau rejected reasoning with children: childhood, he said, is the sleep of reason.",
 v:"Check the section range for the manual trades (§§201–9) and the wording of the wax and white-paper passage (§216)."},

{n:"Rousseau, Emile", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1762", era:"Early modern",
 by:"Jean-Jacques Rousseau", wk:"Emile,_or_On_Education",
 mn:"good", es:"growth", mo:"curiosity", sc:"restricted", au:"teacher", f:"f",
 read:"Book I (opening; man or citizen); Book II (negative education); Book III (Robinson Crusoe); Book V (Sophie)", tr:"Allan Bloom, <i>Emile, or On Education</i> (1979)", ol:[["Barbara Foxley translation (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/5427"]],
 w:"A treatise in the form of a novel. Everything is good as it comes from the hands of the Author of things; everything degenerates in the hands of man. A tutor raises an imaginary boy, Emile, from birth to marriage. The book was condemned and burned in Paris and Geneva the year it appeared.",
 p:"Naturally good, with an original self-love (<i>amour de soi</i>) that society turns into vanity and comparison (<i>amour-propre</i>). The child is not a small adult and has its own ways of seeing, thinking and feeling.",
 e:"A natural man who can live in society without being corrupted by it. Book I poses the choice between making a man and making a citizen, since one cannot do both.",
 x:"In childhood, as little as possible: the first education should be purely negative, preserving the heart from vice and the mind from error rather than teaching virtue or truth. The child learns from the resistance of things, not from words or commands. The only book for the young Emile is <i>Robinson Crusoe</i>.",
 mv:"Curiosity and present usefulness. From about the age of twelve Emile's question about everything is ‘What is it good for?’",
 scp:"Emile's education is for a boy; in Book V Sophie is educated to please him.",
 k:"An all-controlling tutor who arranges the world so that the child believes he is free. There is no subjection so complete, Rousseau writes, as one that keeps the appearance of freedom.",
 wo:"Emile must learn a trade, and Rousseau chooses carpentry: a craft makes a man independent of fortune.",
 mc:"Learning from the resistance of things rather than from words.",
 o:"Mary Wollstonecraft, in <i>A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</i> (1792), attacked Book V, where Sophie is educated only to please and serve Emile. Readers have never forgotten that Rousseau, by his own account in the <i>Confessions</i>, left all five of his children at a foundling hospital."},

{n:"Kant, Enlightenment and Pedagogy", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1784; lectures published 1803", era:"Modern",
 by:"Immanuel Kant", wk:"What_Is_Enlightenment%3F",
 mn:"mixed", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"‘An Answer to the Question: What Is Enlightenment?’ (1784, 8 pages); <i>Lectures on Pedagogy</i>, introduction", tr:"Robert Louden in <i>Anthropology, History, and Education</i> (Cambridge, 2007)", ol:[["What Is Enlightenment?","https://archive.org/details/AnswerTheQuestionWhatIsEnlightenment"],["On Education, Churton translation (Online Library of Liberty)","https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/kant-kant-on-education-uber-padagogik"]],
 w:"Enlightenment is the human being's emergence from self-incurred immaturity, the inability to use one's own understanding without another's guidance. Its motto: have the courage to use your own understanding. The pedagogy lectures, delivered at Königsberg and published by a student, give the programme that produces such a person.",
 p:"The only creature that must be educated. A human being can become human only through education and is nothing but what education makes of him. He has a predisposition to good and a propensity to evil, and is made, as Kant wrote the same year, from crooked timber.",
 e:"Autonomy: a person who gives himself the moral law. Education should aim not at the present state of humanity but at a possibly better future one.",
 x:"Four stages: discipline, which tames savagery; culture, which gives skills; civilization, which gives prudence; and moralization, which gives the disposition to choose good ends. The great problem is how to unite submission under lawful constraint with the capacity to use one's freedom: how to cultivate freedom under constraint.",
 mv:"Courage to use one's own understanding, and later respect for the moral law. Kant distrusts reward as a motive: a child rewarded for doing good will do good only for the reward.",
 scp:"Every human being must be educated, and only through education becomes human.",
 k:"A provisional guardian who must work to become unnecessary. Kant notes the circle: educators were themselves badly educated. He admired Basedow's experimental school at Dessau and wrote in its support.",
 wo:"Children must learn to work: the human being is the only animal that has to, and school is a culture by compulsion to work.",
 mc:"Autonomy: giving oneself the law, the one thing that cannot be done for anyone.",
 o:"J. G. Hamann replied in a letter of December 1784 that Kant blamed the immature for their immaturity and let the guardians, the king among them, off lightly. Hegel and later the communitarians objected that autonomy so defined has no content without the customs of a people.",
 v:"Check the wording of ‘a culture by compulsion’ and of the line on reward against Louden."},

{n:"Wollstonecraft, A Vindication of the Rights of Woman", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1792", era:"Modern",
 by:"Mary Wollstonecraft", wk:"A_Vindication_of_the_Rights_of_Woman",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"Introduction; ch. 2 (against Rousseau's Sophie); ch. 12 (‘On National Education’)", tr:"Original English; ed. Sylvana Tomaselli (Cambridge, 1995)", ol:[["Full text (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3420"]],
 w:"Written in six weeks in reply to the French plan for national education, which offered girls only domestic schooling. It attacks Rousseau's Book V directly and argues that what passes for women's nature is the product of a bad education.",
 p:"A rational being whose reason does not differ by sex. The weakness, vanity and cunning charged against women are an ‘artificial weakness’ produced by an education that trains them to please. Virtue has one standard for both sexes.",
 e:"Rational virtue and independence: women able to be companions rather than ornaments, and mothers and citizens who can think.",
 x:"The exercise of the understanding, against the accomplishments, sentiment and ornament that girls were taught.",
 mv:"Reason and self-respect: a woman must be taught to think for herself, not to please.",
 scp:"Girls and boys, rich and poor, in the same day schools until nine; her whole book is an argument that the mind has no sex.",
 k:"Reason itself, against the authority of custom and of men. National day schools, run by the state, are the institution.",
 wo:"Women need occupations and independence. She suggests they could study medicine, practise as physicians and midwives, and run businesses.",
 mc:"A reason that is one's own, the faculty she insists belongs to women as fully as to men.",
 o:"Her contemporaries mocked her; Horace Walpole called her ‘a hyena in petticoats’. Later feminist readers note how harshly she judged women as they were, and that her national schools still separated children by class after the age of nine.",
 v:"‘Written in six weeks’ is the usual account; confirm. Walpole's phrase comes from a letter of 1795."},

{n:"Fichte, Addresses to the German Nation", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1807–08", era:"Modern",
 by:"Johann Gottlieb Fichte", wk:"Addresses_to_the_German_Nation",
 mn:"neutral", es:"state", mo:"love", sc:"open", au:"state", f:"f",
 read:"Addresses 2 and 3 (the new education); 9–11 (Pestalozzi and national education)", tr:"Gregory Moore, <i>Addresses to the German Nation</i> (Cambridge, 2008)", ol:[["Jones and Turnbull translation, 1922 (Internet Archive)","https://archive.org/details/addressestogerma00fich"]],
 w:"Fourteen lectures given in Berlin in the winter of 1807–08, while the city was occupied by Napoleon's army after Prussia's defeat at Jena. Fichte argued that the nation could be saved only by an entirely new education given to every German child of every class.",
 p:"A will that the old education had left to selfishness, because it merely taught and admonished. The new education must not appeal to a freedom that will go its own way. If you want to influence a person at all, Fichte says in the second address, you must fashion him so that he cannot will otherwise than you wish him to will.",
 e:"A people made one by a single education, and in it a moral will. The end is set by the nation and its renewal.",
 x:"A disposition of the will, formed through the pupil's own activity rather than through information. He took Pestalozzi's method as his model.",
 mv:"Pure love of the good for its own sake. Fichte rejects reward and punishment as motives and wants the pupil's own activity stirred toward pictures of a moral order.",
 scp:"Every German child of every class, in one national education.",
 k:"The state, which must make education national and universal, and teachers who form pupils living apart from adult society in a community of their own.",
 wo:"The pupils' community was to work as well as learn, with agriculture and crafts helping to sustain it.",
 mc:"A love of the good formed through the pupil's own activity rather than imposed by reward.",
 o:"Isaiah Berlin counted Fichte among six enemies of human liberty, and the <i>Addresses</i> were later read as a founding text of German nationalism. Humboldt had argued in the 1790s that public education under the state tends to produce uniform citizens rather than free persons.",
 v:"Confirm that the pupils were to live apart from adult society and that their community was to sustain itself partly by agriculture and crafts (Addresses 2, 3 and 10)."},

{n:"Humboldt and Bildung", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"c. 1792–1810", era:"Modern",
 by:"Wilhelm von Humboldt", wk:"Bildung",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"‘Theory of Bildung’ (fragment, c. 1793); <i>The Limits of State Action</i>, ch. 2; the memorandum on the organization of the Berlin university (1809/10)", tr:"<i>The Limits of State Action</i>, ed. J. W. Burrow (1969); the Bildung fragment in Westbury, Hopmann and Riquarts, eds., <i>Teaching as a Reflective Practice</i> (2000); the Berlin memorandum in <i>Minerva</i> 8 (1970)", ol:[["The Sphere and Duties of Government (Online Library of Liberty)","https://oll.libertyfund.org/titles/589"]],
 w:"The Prussian reformer who founded the University of Berlin in 1810 and gave modern Europe its ideal of <i>Bildung</i>, self-formation through engagement with the world. John Stuart Mill took his epigraph for <i>On Liberty</i> from Humboldt.",
 p:"An individuality whose powers seek development. The true end of man is the highest and most harmonious development of his powers into a complete and consistent whole, and this requires freedom and a variety of situations.",
 e:"<i>Bildung</i> itself: the linking of the self with the world in the freest and most varied interaction.",
 x:"Not a fixed body of knowledge. In the university, science is treated as something never completely found and always to be sought, and teacher and student both serve it. Its conditions are solitude and freedom (<i>Einsamkeit und Freiheit</i>).",
 mv:"The inner urge of each person's powers toward their own development, which needs freedom and a variety of situations.",
 scp:"General education must be the same for the day labourer and the scholar before either specializes (the Lithuanian school plan, 1809).",
 k:"A senior partner in research. The state should protect the university and otherwise stay out of it.",
 wo:"Vocational training comes after general education and must not replace it.",
 mc:"<i>Bildung</i>, the shaping of one's own powers in contact with the world.",
 o:"Nietzsche mocked the cultivated philistine (<i>Bildungsphilister</i>) whom Bildung had become, and sociologists have shown how Bildung turned into the credential of a class. The university Humboldt designed became the model that later, in Lyotard's account, lost its legitimating story.",
 v:"Check the wording of the passage on the day labourer and the scholar in the Lithuanian school plan (1809)."},

{n:"Macaulay, Minute on Indian Education", t:"mod", home:"Colonial India", d:"2 February 1835", era:"Modern",
 by:"Thomas Babington Macaulay", wk:"Minute_on_Indian_Education",
 mn:"neutral", es:"state", mo:"reward", sc:"graded", au:"state", f:"t",
 read:"The Minute itself (about 12 pages); H. T. Prinsep's dissenting note", tr:"Original English, widely reprinted and online", ol:[["The Minute (Frances Pritchett's archive)","https://franpritchett.com/00generallinks/macaulay/txt_minute_education_1835.html"]], ta:["macaulay-minute-on-indian-education","Macaulay, Minute on Indian Education"],
 w:"The Committee of Public Instruction in Bengal was split between ‘Orientalists’, who wanted to fund Sanskrit and Arabic learning, and ‘Anglicists’. Macaulay, newly arrived as Law Member of the Governor-General's council, settled it with a memorandum that Governor-General Bentinck adopted a month later. He admitted he knew neither Sanskrit nor Arabic, and declared that a single shelf of a good European library was worth the whole native literature of India and Arabia.",
 p:"The colonized learner as material for a purpose fixed elsewhere. Macaulay's aim was a class of interpreters between the rulers and the millions they governed: Indian in blood and colour, English in taste, opinions, morals and intellect.",
 e:"A governable empire, and a mediating class that would later refine the vernaculars and pass knowledge down.",
 x:"English and English literature, with Western science.",
 mv:"Advancement. English was the language of the rulers and of government employment.",
 scp:"A class of interpreters, from whom knowledge was to filter down to the masses.",
 k:"The English text and the colonial state that funds it.",
 wo:"English opened government employment, and the system produced clerks for it.",
 mc:"Nothing the Minute names. It wanted interpreters between rulers and ruled.",
 o:"Gandhi's <i>Hind Swaraj</i> (1909) argued that to give millions a knowledge of English is to enslave them. For South Asia the Minute is the origin of the lasting split between the madrasa and the school, and of the question every later entry in this section from the subcontinent tries to answer. It is the clearest case of the modern reversal: the purpose was fixed first, and a person was assumed to fit it."},

{n:"Sir Syed and Aligarh", t:"mod", home:"Islamic, South Asia", d:"1817–1898; college founded 1875", era:"Modern",
 by:"Sayyid Ahmad Khan", wk:"Syed_Ahmad_Khan",
 mn:"neutral", es:"state", mo:"reward", sc:"restricted", au:"teacher", f:"t",
 read:"His essays in <i>Tahzib al-Akhlaq</i> (from 1870) on reason, nature and revelation; the founding documents of the Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College", tr:"Selections in Christian W. Troll, <i>Sayyid Ahmad Khan: A Reinterpretation of Muslim Theology</i> (1978)", ta:["the-muhammadan-anglo-oriental-college-aligarh","The Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College, Aligarh"],
 w:"After 1857 Sir Syed concluded that Indian Muslims would be ruined unless they took up English education and modern science. He visited England in 1869–70, founded a reformist journal named, after Miskawayh, <i>Tahzib al-Akhlaq</i>, and in 1875 founded the college at Aligarh that became a university in 1920.",
 p:"A rational believer for whom the Word of God and the Work of God, revelation and nature, cannot contradict each other. His opponents called his followers <i>necharis</i>, ‘naturists’.",
 e:"A Muslim community able to compete in the colonial order, with its faith reinterpreted in harmony with natural science.",
 x:"English and Western sciences, with religious instruction alongside.",
 mv:"The survival and advancement of the community within the colonial order, through posts, professions and respect.",
 scp:"Sons of respectable Muslim families; he held that women's schooling should wait until men were educated.",
 k:"The modern college, staffed partly by English teachers, under Muslim patronage.",
 wo:"The college was built to bring Muslims back into the professions and the administration.",
 mc:"Reason reading nature and revelation together, which he held could never truly conflict.",
 o:"The Deoband seminary, founded in 1866, took the opposite path, preserving the inherited curriculum outside the state. Jamāl al-Dīn al-Afghānī's <i>Refutation of the Materialists</i> (1881) attacked Sir Syed's naturism. The poet Akbar Allahabadi spent a career satirizing the Aligarh graduate. Iqbal took up the question a generation later.",
 v:"Find a source for his view that women's schooling should wait until men were educated."},

{n:"Newman, The Idea of a University", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"lectures 1852; book 1873", era:"Modern",
 by:"John Henry Newman", wk:"The_Idea_of_a_University",
 mn:"mixed", es:"within", mo:"curiosity", sc:"graded", au:"tradition", f:"f",
 read:"Preface; Discourses V (‘Knowledge Its Own End’), VI–VII, and VIII (the gentleman)", tr:"Original English; ed. Frank M. Turner (1996)", ol:[["Full text (Newman Reader)","https://www.newmanreader.org/works/idea/"]],
 w:"Lectures given in Dublin when Newman was rector of a new Catholic university. They are the classic defence of liberal education against the utilitarians of the <i>Edinburgh Review</i>, who asked what learning was for.",
 p:"An intellect that can be cultivated for its own sake, and that is distinct from conscience and holiness. Newman is clear that liberal education makes not the Christian but the gentleman. To expect knowledge to subdue the passions is, in his image, to quarry granite with razors or moor a ship with a thread of silk.",
 e:"A philosophical habit of mind, marked by freedom, equitableness, calmness, moderation and wisdom.",
 x:"Universal knowledge, theology included, since a university that omits any branch distorts the rest. The university's object is intellectual, not moral, and its work is teaching; research belongs to academies.",
 mv:"The mind's desire for its own excellence. Knowledge, he argues, is its own end, and that is reason enough to pursue it.",
 scp:"Those able to receive a liberal education in a residential university.",
 k:"A university as a community of teachers and students, more than its formal instruction. Newman said that if he had to choose between a university that gave degrees by examination without residence and one that simply gathered young men together for three or four years, he would choose the second.",
 wo:"Liberal knowledge is distinct from useful knowledge. A liberal education does not train for a trade, though it makes a person fitter for any.",
 mc:"A philosophical habit of mind: seeing things in their relations, which no store of information supplies.",
 o:"The utilitarians he was answering, and the Humboldtian research university that soon made his separation of teaching from research obsolete. A religious objection also follows from his own premise: if knowledge cannot make anyone good, why should the Church found universities?"},

{n:"Nietzsche, Schopenhauer as Educator", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1872–1874", era:"Modern",
 by:"Friedrich Nietzsche", wk:"Untimely_Meditations",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"chosen", mo:"will", sc:"graded", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"‘Schopenhauer as Educator’ (Untimely Meditations III), §§1–3; ‘On the Future of Our Educational Institutions’ (1872), lecture 1", tr:"R. J. Hollingdale, <i>Untimely Meditations</i> (1983); Damion Searls, <i>Anti-Education</i> (2016)", ol:[["Thoughts out of Season, Part II (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/38226"]],
 w:"Two early works by a young professor at Basel. The lectures attack the German gymnasium and university for serving the state and journalism. The essay asks what a true educator is, and answers with the figure who first showed Nietzsche what philosophy could be.",
 p:"A self that is a task. The conscience says: be yourself; all you are now doing, thinking and desiring is not you. Your true nature lies not hidden deep within you but immeasurably high above you.",
 e:"Culture, whose goal is the production of genius, the higher human being; for the individual, becoming who one is.",
 x:"An example to follow and then outgrow. Your true educators can be nothing other than your liberators.",
 mv:"The call of conscience: be yourself. An educator awakens the will to climb toward the self that lies above you.",
 scp:"Culture exists for the production of the rare great individual.",
 k:"An exemplar one eventually leaves. Zarathustra later tells his disciples that one repays a teacher badly by always remaining a pupil.",
 wo:"He attacked the schools for producing scholars and journalists to serve the state's needs.",
 mc:"Becoming who one is, which no system can do on anyone's behalf.",
 o:"The democratic objection, from Dewey among others: education for genius abandons the many. Nietzsche himself later broke with Schopenhauer, which shows how the doctrine works in practice."},

{n:"Washington, Up from Slavery", t:"mod", home:"American", d:"1895–1901", era:"Modern",
 by:"Booker T. Washington", wk:"Up_from_Slavery",
 mn:"neutral", es:"state", mo:"reward", sc:"open", au:"teacher", f:"b",
 read:"<i>Up from Slavery</i>, chs. 3–6 and 14 (the Atlanta Exposition address)", tr:"Original English", ol:[["Full text (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2376"]],
 w:"The autobiography of a man born into slavery who founded the Tuskegee Institute in Alabama in 1881. His Atlanta Exposition address of 1895 made him the most influential Black American of his generation.",
 p:"A people held back less by nature than by a lack of skills, property and habits of work, which slavery had denied them.",
 e:"Economic independence and the respect that follows it, within the social order of the South as it then was.",
 x:"Trades, agriculture and habits: cleanliness, thrift and work. At Tuskegee students built the school's buildings with bricks they made themselves.",
 mv:"Usefulness and the respect it earns. A people that can do something the world needs, he argued, will not be shut out for long.",
 scp:"The mass of freed people and their children, most of whom had been forbidden by law to read under slavery.",
 k:"The institution and its founder, working within limits set by the white South.",
 wo:"Industrial education at the centre: trades, farming and the dignity of labour. No race can prosper, he said at Atlanta in 1895, until it learns that there is as much dignity in tilling a field as in writing a poem.",
 mc:"The dignity of labour, which belongs to the worker and not to the product.",
 o:"Du Bois, in <i>The Souls of Black Folk</i> (1903), charged that Washington asked Black Americans to give up, for the present, political power, the insistence on civil rights and the higher education of their youth."},

{n:"Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk", t:"mod", home:"American", d:"1903", era:"Modern",
 by:"W. E. B. Du Bois", wk:"The_Souls_of_Black_Folk",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"graded", au:"teacher", f:"f",
 read:"Chs. 3 (‘Of Mr. Booker T. Washington and Others’), 5 (‘Of the Wings of Atalanta’) and 6 (‘Of the Training of Black Men’); with the essay ‘The Talented Tenth’ (1903)", tr:"Original English", ol:[["Full text (Standard Ebooks)","https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/w-e-b-du-bois/the-souls-of-black-folk/text/single-page"]],
 w:"A book of essays by the first Black American to earn a Harvard doctorate. It introduced the idea of double consciousness and opened the public argument with Washington over what education a people just out of slavery needed first.",
 p:"A people whose gifts are equal to any, seen by others and forced to see itself through a veil.",
 e:"Full humanity, which he called manhood: intelligence, broad sympathy and knowledge of the world, and the leadership to win civil rights.",
 x:"Liberal culture, in colleges, as well as trades in schools.",
 mv:"Aspiration toward the highest culture, and the refusal to accept a lower place.",
 scp:"The ‘talented tenth’, educated in colleges to lead and lift the rest; he later revised the idea as too narrow.",
 k:"The college and the educated leader.",
 wo:"The college must not only teach bread-winning; it must be the place where knowledge of life is joined to life.",
 mc:"Culture, broad sympathy and knowledge of the world, which he called the true object of the schools.",
 o:"Washington's side held that a few college graduates could not lift a people without an economic base beneath them. Du Bois himself revised the idea in 1948, criticizing the talented tenth for its elitism."},

{n:"Weber, Science as a Vocation", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"lecture 1917; published 1919", era:"Modern",
 by:"Max Weber", wk:"Science_as_a_Vocation",
 mn:"neutral", es:"chosen", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"t",
 read:"The whole lecture (about 40 pages)", tr:"Rodney Livingstone, in <i>The Vocation Lectures</i> (2004)",
 w:"A lecture to students in Munich during the war. The fate of the age, Weber says, is rationalization, intellectualization and the disenchantment of the world. Science can tell us how to master life technically; it cannot answer Tolstoy's question, what shall we do and how shall we live.",
 p:"An adult who must choose among warring values, the ‘gods’ of a polytheism that reason cannot settle, and bear responsibility for the choice.",
 e:"Clarity, and an account of oneself: knowing the consequences of one's commitments.",
 x:"Specialized knowledge, methods, ‘inconvenient facts’, and clarity about means and consequences. Not a view of life.",
 mv:"Vocation and passion. Without the passion that stakes everything on getting one conjecture right, Weber says, a person has no calling for science.",
 scp:"Any student, though few have the vocation.",
 k:"A specialist, not a leader or prophet. Students in a lecture hall must be silent and cannot answer back, so the professor who preaches there abuses his position. Youth, Weber says, are looking for leaders and are given teachers.",
 wo:"Science has become a profession, and the academic is a state employee, separated like a worker from the means of production.",
 mc:"Choosing among ultimate values, which science cannot do for anyone. A system that answers how cannot answer which.",
 o:"Leo Strauss, in <i>Natural Right and History</i> (1953), argued that Weber's separation of facts from values is itself a value judgment and leads to nihilism. For this atlas, Weber is the moment formation is formally expelled from the university.",
 v:"The passage on passion is paraphrased; check against Livingstone."},

{n:"Dewey, Democracy and Education", t:"mod", home:"American", d:"1916", era:"Modern",
 by:"John Dewey", wk:"Democracy_and_Education",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"growth", mo:"curiosity", sc:"open", au:"teacher", f:"b",
 read:"Chs. 1, 4 (‘Education as Growth’), 6–8, 24; with <i>Experience and Education</i> (1938)", tr:"Original English", ol:[["Full text (Project Gutenberg)","https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/852"]],
 w:"Dewey's systematic statement, written with Peirce's pragmatism behind it. Philosophy itself, he says near the end, can be defined as the general theory of education. In 1919–21 he lectured in China for two years, with Hu Shih interpreting, one of the few documented meetings of two of the traditions in this atlas.",
 p:"A living organism in an environment, social through and through. Immaturity is not a lack but a positive power to grow, and dependence and plasticity are capacities (ch. 4).",
 e:"Growth, which has no end beyond itself: since growth is the characteristic of life, education is all one with growing. Democracy, as a form of associated living, is the society in which such growth is shared.",
 x:"Experience reconstructed so that it adds to the meaning of experience and to the ability to direct later experience. Society exists through the transmission of habits, ideas and practices, and transmission happens through communication.",
 mv:"Interest: the learner's own engagement in an activity whose outcome he cares about. Effort imposed without interest, Dewey argues, produces divided attention.",
 scp:"Every child, in a common school that a democracy shares.",
 k:"The teacher arranges the environment and takes part in the shared activity. Aims are internal to activities, not imposed from outside. In 1938 Dewey criticized progressive schools that took ‘freedom’ to mean the absence of direction.",
 wo:"Occupations such as gardening, cooking, weaving and carpentry belong at the centre of the curriculum, and liberal and vocational education should not be split (ch. 23).",
 mc:"Intelligence as inquiry in a live situation: reconstructing experience by acting on it.",
 o:"Robert Maynard Hutchins argued for a fixed curriculum of great books because human nature is the same everywhere. Arendt's ‘Crisis in Education’ was aimed in part at what American schools had made of Dewey."},

{n:"Tagore, The Parrot's Training", t:"mod", home:"Indian", d:"1918; Visva-Bharati founded 1921", era:"Modern",
 by:"Rabindranath Tagore", wk:"Rabindranath_Tagore",
 mn:"good", es:"within", mo:"curiosity", sc:"open", au:"teacher", f:"f",
 read:"‘The Parrot's Training’ (a short fable); ‘My School’ in <i>Personality</i> (1917)", tr:"Original Bengali and Tagore's own English", ol:[["The Parrot's Training (Internet Archive)","https://archive.org/details/ParrotsTraining-RabindranathTagore"]], ta:["tagores-santiniketan","Tagore's Santiniketan"],
 w:"A king decides his parrot needs education. Pundits build a golden cage, scribes copy texts and stuff the pages into its beak, and officials report progress. The bird dies, and the king's nephews announce that its education is complete. Tagore had founded a school at Santiniketan in 1901, with classes under trees and teaching in Bengali, and in 1921 the university Visva-Bharati.",
 p:"A child whose creative spirit is in natural kinship with the world, and whom schooling cages and starves.",
 e:"A life in harmony with all existence, which he said was the highest education, beyond information. A universal person rooted in a particular culture.",
 x:"Nature, art, music, a mother tongue, and the meeting of cultures.",
 mv:"Joy and freedom, the child's natural delight in the world, which the cage of schooling kills.",
 scp:"Every child, in a school open to nature and the village.",
 k:"A companion who lives alongside the students, in a place that itself teaches.",
 wo:"At Sriniketan (1922) he joined the school to village crafts and agriculture.",
 mc:"Joy and kinship with the world, which a cage of instruction kills.",
 o:"In 1921 Gandhi called on students to leave government schools. Tagore objected in ‘The Call of Truth’ that non-cooperation was a creed of negation, and Gandhi replied in ‘The Great Sentinel’. Gandhi's own scheme of Nai Talim (1937) made a productive craft the centre of schooling and made schools pay for themselves, a sharper break with colonial education than Tagore's.",
 v:"The fable is dated 1918 here; confirm the date of the Bengali original."},

{n:"Ambedkar, Annihilation of Caste", t:"mod", home:"Indian", d:"1936", era:"Modern",
 by:"B. R. Ambedkar", wk:"Annihilation_of_Caste",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"The undelivered address, especially the sections on caste and the division of labour and on the authority of the śāstras; Gandhi's reply in <i>Harijan</i> and Ambedkar's rejoinder", tr:"Original English; annotated critical edition by S. Anand (Navayana, 2014)", ta:["ambedkar-annihilation-of-caste","Ambedkar, Annihilation of Caste"],
 w:"Written as the presidential address to an anti-caste society in Lahore, which withdrew the invitation when it read the text. Ambedkar published it himself. Its argument is that caste cannot be reformed from within Hinduism while the scriptures that sanction it keep their authority.",
 p:"A person equal in worth to any other, whose capacities caste denies by assigning a place at birth. Ambedkar himself was made to sit apart in school and was refused water from the common tap.",
 e:"A society of liberty, equality and fraternity, and in it persons who can choose their own path.",
 x:"Reason and knowledge that had been forbidden: the very learning the old order reserved for the few.",
 mv:"Self-respect. His slogan to the depressed classes in 1942 was ‘Educate, agitate, organise’.",
 scp:"Against a social order that forbade the śūdra and the untouchable to learn at all; education is the first demand of the excluded.",
 k:"Not scripture. He argues that the śāstras must lose their authority, and that each person must judge them by reason and morality. He later founded the People's Education Society (1945) and Siddharth College in Bombay (1946).",
 wo:"Caste is not merely a division of labour but a division of labourers into watertight compartments, fixed by birth rather than aptitude or choice.",
 mc:"Choosing one's own work by capacity. Any system that assigns people to tasks by origin repeats what he opposed in caste.",
 o:"Gandhi replied in <i>Harijan</i> (1936), condemning untouchability while defending varṇa as a division of hereditary callings; Ambedkar answered that the defence of varṇa was the defence of caste. The quarrel between them is the sharpest modern Indian form of the question of who may learn."},

{n:"Gandhi, Basic Education (Nai Talim)", t:"mod", home:"Indian", d:"1937", era:"Modern",
 by:"M. K. Gandhi; scheme drafted by a committee under Zakir Husain", wk:"Nai_Talim",
 mn:"good", es:"within", mo:"reverence", sc:"open", au:"tradition", f:"f",
 read:"Gandhi's <i>Harijan</i> articles of July to October 1937; the Wardha conference resolutions and the Zakir Husain committee report (1938)", tr:"Original English and Hindustani; Gandhi, <i>Basic Education</i> (Navajivan, 1951)", ol:[["Gandhi on basic education (mkgandhi.org)","https://www.mkgandhi.org/voiceoftruth/basiceducation.php"]], ta:["the-wardha-scheme","The Wardha Scheme"],
 w:"Gandhi proposed in 1937 that primary education be built around a productive craft and be self-supporting. A conference at Wardha accepted the scheme, and a committee chaired by Zakir Husain of Jamia Millia, later President of India, drafted its curriculum.",
 p:"Body, mind and spirit together. By education, Gandhi wrote, he meant an all-round drawing out of the best in child and man, and literacy is not the end of education nor even its beginning.",
 e:"Self-reliant persons in self-reliant villages, and a society that does not live by exploitation.",
 x:"A craft and, through it, arithmetic, language, history and science, learned in use.",
 mv:"The discipline and dignity of productive work, done from the first day of school.",
 scp:"Every child, seven years free and compulsory, in the mother tongue, in the village.",
 k:"The village community, the craft and the teacher as fellow worker; in the mother tongue, not English.",
 wo:"The centre of the school: a productive craft such as spinning, through which every other subject is taught, with the school paying for itself from what the children make.",
 mc:"The hand. In <i>Hind Swaraj</i> (1909) he called machinery the chief symbol of modern civilization.",
 o:"Critics argued that the scheme would tie village children to village crafts while others went on to modern professions, and the planners of independent India favoured industrial and technical education; the scheme faded after 1947. Tagore's objection, that a school built on production narrows the child's world, is the older one."},

{n:"Iqbal, Reconstruction and Khudi", t:"mod", home:"Islamic, South Asia", d:"1915–1936", era:"Modern",
 by:"Muhammad Iqbal", wk:"The_Reconstruction_of_Religious_Thought_in_Islam",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"<i>The Reconstruction of Religious Thought in Islam</i>, lectures 1, 4 and 5; <i>Asrar-i-Khudi</i> (1915); the section ‘Taleem o Tarbiyat’ in <i>Zarb-i-Kalim</i> (1936)", tr:"<i>Reconstruction</i> in Iqbal's English (1930/1934); <i>Asrar</i> trans. R. A. Nicholson, <i>The Secrets of the Self</i> (1920)", ol:[["The Reconstruction (Iqbal Cyber Library)","https://iqbalcyberlibrary.net/en/342.html"]], ta:["iqbal-on-schooling","Iqbal on schooling"],
 w:"The poet-philosopher who sought to reconstruct Islamic thought with Bergson, Nietzsche and the Qur'an in view, and who gave Pakistan its founding vision. In verse he attacked both the madrasa, for producing narrow clerics, and the modern school, for producing servile clerks; both, he thought, killed the self.",
 p:"An ego (<i>khudi</i>), finite, free and creative, a co-worker with God. The ego's aim, he says in lecture 4, is not to see something but to be something. The self is not given complete: it is achieved through striving, and it can weaken and dissolve.",
 e:"The strengthening of selfhood toward God. <i>Asrar</i> names three stages: obedience to law, self-control, and divine vicegerency (<i>niyābat-i ilāhī</i>).",
 x:"Not information but vitality: an education that hardens the self rather than dissolves it. In lecture 5 he argues that the finality of prophethood frees human reason to stand on its own, and that the birth of Islam was the birth of the inductive intellect.",
 mv:"Desire and love (<i>ʿishq</i>). In the <i>Asrar</i> life is kept alive by desire, and love strengthens the self in its striving toward God.",
 scp:"Every self can be strengthened or weakened.",
 k:"The perfect believer (<i>mard-i momin</i>), and Rumi as guide, with Iqbal as his Indian disciple. Ijtihad, independent reasoning, is to be renewed by legislative assemblies rather than individuals (lecture 6).",
 wo:"Action is the test of the self; he condemned idleness and dependence.",
 mc:"<i>Khudi</i>, a self that must be achieved; his warning was against any education that makes the self passive.",
 o:"Traditionalists read <i>khudi</i> as Nietzschean individualism in Islamic dress; Nicholson's introduction compared the two, and Iqbal protested. The reverse objection is that his synthesis gave a modern state a heroic language for education without a curriculum."},

{n:"Sartre, Existentialism Is a Humanism", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"lecture 1945; published 1946", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Jean-Paul Sartre", wk:"Existentialism_Is_a_Humanism",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"chosen", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"The whole lecture (about 40 pages), especially the passage on existence and essence and the story of the student", tr:"Carol Macomber, <i>Existentialism Is a Humanism</i> (Yale, 2007), or Philip Mairet (1948)", ol:[["Full text (Marxists Internet Archive)","https://www.marxists.org/reference/archive/sartre/works/exist/sartre.htm"]],
 w:"A public lecture given in Paris in October 1945 to an overflowing crowd, answering Catholic and Communist critics of existentialism. It became the best-known statement of the movement, and Sartre later had reservations about it.",
 p:"There is no human nature, since there is no God to have conceived it. Existence precedes essence: a human being first exists, encounters himself and surges up in the world, and only afterwards defines himself. Man is nothing other than what he makes of himself.",
 e:"None given. Each person invents values by choosing and is responsible for what he becomes.",
 x:"Nothing that settles a life. A former student came to Sartre during the war unable to decide whether to join the Free French or stay with his mother, who lived for him alone. Sartre's answer: you are free, so choose, that is, invent. No general ethic can decide for you.",
 mv:"Freedom, and the anguish of choosing for oneself and, by implication, for everyone.",
 scp:"Every human being is condemned to be free.",
 k:"No teacher can decide. To choose an adviser, Sartre says, is already to have chosen, since one knows roughly in advance what each adviser will say.",
 wo:"A person is the sum of his actions; there is no reality except in action.",
 mc:"Choice under anguish. No adviser and no system can choose in the student's place.",
 o:"Iris Murdoch, in ‘The Idea of Perfection’ (1964), argued that this picture of an empty, choosing will misdescribes moral life, which is mostly a matter of attention, of learning to see others justly before any choice arrives. That is an account of moral education Sartre's picture leaves no room for. Heidegger's ‘Letter on Humanism’ (1947) rejected Sartre's reading of his work."},

{n:"Arendt, The Crisis in Education", t:"mod", home:"European-American", d:"1954; in <i>Between Past and Future</i> (1961)", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Hannah Arendt", wk:"Between_Past_and_Future",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"chosen", mo:"love", sc:"open", au:"teacher", f:"b",
 read:"The whole essay (about 25 pages)", tr:"Original English",
 w:"Written about American schools, the essay argues that a crisis in education reveals a crisis of the modern world: the loss of authority and of tradition. Arendt attacks three assumptions of progressive education: a separate world of children governed by children; a science of teaching in general, detached from any subject; and the replacement of learning by doing.",
 p:"A newcomer. The essence of education is natality, the fact that human beings are born into the world. The child is new in an old world and needs protection from it, and the world needs protection from the new.",
 e:"To preserve the newness of each child so that they can one day renew a common world. Education must be conservative precisely so that politics can be revolutionary.",
 x:"Knowledge of the world as it is. The teacher is qualified by knowing the world and by being able to instruct others about it.",
 mv:"Love of the world on the teacher's side, which takes responsibility for it; on the child's side, the newness each child brings and must be free to use.",
 scp:"Every child, as a newcomer.",
 k:"The teacher's authority rests on responsibility for the world. Education is the point, Arendt writes, at which we decide whether we love the world enough to assume responsibility for it. Educating adults is not education but indoctrination; education has no place in politics.",
 wo:"In <i>The Human Condition</i> (1958) she distinguishes labour, work and action; in schools she opposed replacing learning by doing.",
 mc:"Newness: each child's capacity to begin something no one foresaw.",
 o:"Progressive educators answer that her ‘world’ is someone's world, inherited unequally. Her separate essay on school desegregation, ‘Reflections on Little Rock’ (1959), was attacked by Ralph Ellison and others for misunderstanding what Black parents were asking of schools."},

{n:"Schultz and Becker, Human Capital", t:"mod", home:"American", d:"1961–1964", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Theodore W. Schultz and Gary S. Becker", wk:"Human_capital",
 mn:"neutral", es:"market", mo:"reward", sc:"open", au:"state", f:"t",
 read:"Schultz, ‘Investment in Human Capital’, <i>American Economic Review</i> 51 (1961), the whole address; Becker, <i>Human Capital</i> (1964), the introduction and the chapters on rates of return", tr:"Original English",
 w:"Schultz's presidential address to the American Economic Association, delivered in December 1960, argued that much of the growth in national income that could not be explained by more land, labour and physical capital came from investment in people: schooling, training and health. Becker's book gave the idea a formal theory and measured education by its rate of return. Schultz received the Nobel Prize in economics in 1979 and Becker in 1992, and the idea became the working language of development agencies.",
 p:"A bearer of productive capacities who invests in himself. Laborers, Schultz writes, have become capitalists, not through owning shares but through acquiring knowledge and skill that have economic value. In Becker's wider economic approach the person is a rational maximizer of expected benefit.",
 e:"Productivity and growth, for the individual and the nation. The end is set by the market.",
 x:"Skills that raise productivity. What cannot be priced does not enter the account.",
 mv:"The expected return: higher earnings, set against the cost of schooling and the income forgone while studying.",
 scp:"Everyone, as an investment, with returns compared across levels of schooling.",
 k:"Whoever produces the skill most efficiently, judged by returns. Schultz acknowledged that treating human beings as wealth to be increased by investment runs against deeply held values.",
 wo:"Learning is livelihood: schooling is valued by the earnings it adds.",
 mc:"Whatever skills the market still pays for. On its own terms the theory has no other answer.",
 o:"Michael Spence's signalling model (1973) argued that schooling may raise earnings by certifying ability rather than creating it, and Randall Collins's <i>The Credential Society</i> (1979) traced the inflation of degrees that follows. Bowles and Gintis, in <i>Schooling in Capitalist America</i> (1976), argued that schools reproduce class. In this atlas the theory is the plainest case of a purpose fixed outside the learner, with a person assumed to fit it.",
 v:"Check the wording of Schultz's remark that treating human beings as wealth runs against deeply held values."},

{n:"Freire, Pedagogy of the Oppressed", t:"mod", home:"Latin American", d:"1968; English 1970", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Paulo Freire", wk:"Pedagogy_of_the_Oppressed",
 mn:"unfinished", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"Chs. 1–3", tr:"Myra Bergman Ramos (1970; 50th anniversary ed. 2018)",
 w:"Written in exile after the 1964 coup in Brazil by an educator who had taught adult peasants to read through words drawn from their own lives. Its second chapter names the ‘banking’ concept of education, in which the teacher deposits and the students receive, file and store.",
 p:"An unfinished, historical being whose vocation is to become more fully human. Oppression dehumanizes both oppressor and oppressed, and the oppressed carry the oppressor's image inside themselves.",
 e:"Liberation through <i>conscientização</i>, critical consciousness of one's situation that issues in action to change it.",
 x:"Not deposits but problems. The educator poses the learners' own situation as a problem through ‘generative themes’, and knowledge arises in dialogue and praxis, reflection and action together.",
 mv:"The learners' own interest in becoming more fully human, awakened when their situation is posed to them as a problem they can act on.",
 scp:"The oppressed above all, who were denied the word.",
 k:"There are no longer teacher and students but a teacher-student and student-teachers, each learning from the others. Dialogue requires love, humility and faith in people.",
 wo:"Literacy built from the words of peasants' own work and lives.",
 mc:"Critical consciousness: reading the world as well as the word.",
 o:"Peter Berger, in <i>Pyramids of Sacrifice</i> (1974), argued that ‘consciousness-raising’ presumes the educator knows better than the people what their consciousness should be. The teacher's own direction, critics say, is still there; it has only become harder to argue with."},

{n:"Illich, Deschooling Society", t:"mod", home:"European-Latin American", d:"1971", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Ivan Illich", wk:"Deschooling_Society",
 mn:"neutral", es:"chosen", mo:"curiosity", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"Chs. 1, 2 and 6 (‘Learning Webs’); with <i>Tools for Conviviality</i> (1973)", tr:"Original English",
 w:"A priest and former university vice-rector, writing from his centre at Cuernavaca, argued that compulsory schooling had become a ritual and a monopoly. The pupil is schooled to confuse teaching with learning, promotion with education, a diploma with competence, and fluency with the ability to say something new.",
 p:"A convivial person who learns most of what he knows outside school, casually and by taking part in life. Institutions turn needs into demands for their own services.",
 e:"Autonomy and conviviality: a society whose tools serve persons rather than the reverse.",
 x:"Skills and knowledge freely sought. In place of schools he proposed ‘learning webs’: reference services to educational objects, skill exchanges, peer matching, and directories of educators.",
 mv:"Curiosity freely followed. Most learning, Illich argues, happens casually, as a by-product of taking part in life.",
 scp:"Anyone, freed from compulsory schooling.",
 k:"Masters, peers and skill models, freely chosen, with no compulsory credential. Later in life Illich turned to the history of reading in Hugh of St Victor, and said he had underestimated how deeply schooling shapes perception itself.",
 wo:"Skill exchanges, in which people who can do something teach it to those who want to learn, without credentials.",
 mc:"Conviviality. In <i>Tools for Conviviality</i> (1973) he argued that tools past a certain scale make people their servants.",
 o:"Herbert Gintis replied in the <i>Harvard Educational Review</i> (1972) that without schools the poor would be left to markets and to the unequal resources of their families. Market reformers later took up ‘deschooling’ in ways Illich disowned."},

{n:"Dreyfus, What Computers Can't Do", t:"mod", home:"American", d:"1972; with Stuart Dreyfus, <i>Mind over Machine</i>, 1986", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Hubert L. Dreyfus", wk:"Hubert_Dreyfus%27s_views_on_artificial_intelligence",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"teacher", f:"f",
 read:"<i>What Computers Can't Do</i>, introduction and part III; <i>Mind over Machine</i>, ch. 1 (the five stages of skill); <i>On the Internet</i> (2001), ch. 2 on distance learning", tr:"Original English",
 w:"A philosopher trained in Heidegger and Merleau-Ponty, Dreyfus argued at the height of early artificial intelligence that its programme of reducing intelligence to rules would fail. With his brother Stuart he later described how people learn skills, from novice to expert.",
 p:"An embodied being who copes with a world of meanings through practical know-how, most of which cannot be made explicit.",
 e:"Expertise and practical wisdom: responding to each situation as it is, without consulting rules.",
 x:"Skill, passed on by example, practice and correction, in which rules are a beginner's crutch that the expert leaves behind.",
 mv:"Involvement: caring about the outcome and risking failure, which he argued is what moves a learner from competence to expertise.",
 scp:"Anyone who learns a skill by practice under a master.",
 k:"The master in an apprenticeship. In <i>On the Internet</i> he argued that distance learning cannot produce expertise because it removes risk and involvement.",
 wo:"Expertise is learned through apprenticeship and involved practice, in stages from novice to expert, not by applying rules.",
 mc:"This is the text's own question. Skilled human knowing rests on an embodied background of know-how that cannot be written down as rules, and that background, he argued, is what machines lack.",
 o:"Systems that learn from examples rather than rules have since done much of what he said computers could not do, which weakens his predictions. His defenders reply that his account of embodied, involved learning was never only a prediction about machines, and it remains a description of how people become expert."},

{n:"Rancière, The Ignorant Schoolmaster", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1987; English 1991", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Jacques Rancière", wk:"The_Ignorant_Schoolmaster",
 mn:"neutral", es:"chosen", mo:"will", sc:"open", au:"self", f:"f",
 read:"Chs. 1–2 (‘An Intellectual Adventure’, ‘The Ignorant One's Lesson’)", tr:"Kristin Ross, <i>The Ignorant Schoolmaster</i> (1991)",
 w:"In 1818 Joseph Jacotot, a French exile teaching at Leuven, had Flemish students who spoke no French and he no Flemish. He gave them a bilingual edition of Fénelon's <i>Télémaque</i> and had them learn the French through the translation. They learned to write French well. Jacotot concluded that one can teach what one does not know.",
 p:"Every person has equal intelligence. What differs is will and attention. Equality is not a goal education reaches but a starting assumption it verifies.",
 e:"Intellectual emancipation: awareness of what one's own intelligence can do.",
 x:"Nothing explained. Explanation, Rancière argues, is the myth of pedagogy: it creates the incapacity it claims to remedy, by teaching the pupil that he cannot understand without an explainer. Jacotot called the alternative ‘universal teaching’.",
 mv:"The will. What differs between people, Rancière argues, is not intelligence but will and attention, and the master's task is to keep the pupil's will at work.",
 scp:"Everyone, since all intelligence is equal.",
 k:"A master of the will, not of knowledge, who obliges the pupil to use his own intelligence and checks that he has done so. Ignorance can be an advantage.",
 wo:"His earlier book, <i>The Nights of Labor</i> (1981), studies nineteenth-century workers who taught themselves philosophy and poetry at night.",
 mc:"The will to use one's own intelligence. An explanation can be automated; the decision to think cannot.",
 o:"The book in Jacotot's experiment was written and translated by people who knew; the knowledge was in the book. And universal teaching, Rancière reports, became a method, a fashion, and then an institution, the fate he says emancipation always meets."},

{n:"Lyotard, The Postmodern Condition", t:"mod", home:"European", d:"1979; English 1984", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Jean-François Lyotard", wk:"The_Postmodern_Condition",
 mn:"neutral", es:"market", mo:"reward", sc:"open", au:"state", f:"t",
 read:"Introduction; §§9–12 (the narratives of legitimation and their decline; performativity and education)", tr:"Geoff Bennington and Brian Massumi (1984)",
 w:"A report on knowledge in the most developed societies, written for the universities council of Quebec. Two grand stories once justified the university: the speculative story of spirit and Bildung (Humboldt and Hegel), and the story of the people's emancipation. Both, Lyotard argues, have lost their credibility.",
 p:"Less a self than a post in a network: each person exists in a fabric of relations, a point through which messages pass.",
 e:"Performativity: optimizing the system's input–output ratio.",
 x:"Competences the system needs. The question asked of knowledge shifts from ‘is it true?’ to ‘what use is it?’, and then to ‘is it saleable?’ and ‘is it efficient?’ Transmission of established knowledge, he predicts, can be handed to machines, and teachers will be needed only to teach how to use the data banks.",
 mv:"Performance: the efficiency of the system and the learner's place in it.",
 scp:"Mass higher education, organized to supply competences.",
 k:"Whoever, or whatever, transmits most efficiently. Lyotard set against this a ‘paralogy’ of inventive disagreement.",
 wo:"Knowledge becomes a commodity produced to be sold, and universities train the competences the system needs.",
 mc:"Lyotard's own answer is paralogy: inventing new moves that break the rules of a game, which a system built for performance resists.",
 o:"Jürgen Habermas, in ‘Modernity: An Incomplete Project’ (1980), defended the Enlightenment's claims to reason and consensus. Read in 2026, the report's prediction about machines needs no commentary; its account of legitimation is the part still to be answered."},

{n:"Fazlur Rahman, Islam and Modernity", t:"mod", home:"Islamic, South Asia", d:"1982", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Fazlur Rahman", wk:"Fazlur_Rahman_Malik",
 mn:"mixed", es:"within", mo:"reverence", sc:"open", au:"tradition", f:"b",
 read:"<i>Islam and Modernity: Transformation of an Intellectual Tradition</i>, chs. 1–3 and the conclusion", tr:"Original English", ta:["fazlur-rahman-at-the-islamic-research-institute","Fazlur Rahman at the Islamic Research Institute"],
 w:"A historian of Islamic thought who directed Pakistan's Islamic Research Institute until he was forced out in 1968, and then taught at Chicago. The book traces how madrasa learning narrowed into commentaries on commentaries, how the Dars-i Nizami of eighteenth-century Lucknow gave logic and theology more space than the Qur'an, and how colonialism added a secular system beside it without joining the two.",
 p:"A moral agent addressed by a Qur'an whose purpose is ethical. Its central quality is <i>taqwā</i>, conscientious awareness of standing before God.",
 e:"A renewed Islamic intellectualism able to think through the modern world rather than merely react to it.",
 x:"A way of reading. His ‘double movement’ goes from a Qur'anic ruling back to its historical situation to find its moral principle, then forward to apply that principle in the present.",
 mv:"<i>Taqwā</i>, the conscientious awareness of standing before God, which he placed at the centre of the Qur'an's moral teaching.",
 scp:"Every Muslim, in an education that joins faith and the modern disciplines.",
 k:"Scholars trained in both history and hermeneutics. He doubted that knowledge could be made Islamic by relabelling Western disciplines; it had to come from minds formed by the Qur'an's ethic.",
 wo:"He blamed the split between madrasa and modern school, in which neither trains minds able to think about faith and the world together.",
 mc:"The double movement: finding the moral principle behind a ruling and applying it anew.",
 o:"Traditional ʿulamāʾ in Pakistan accused him of historicizing revelation, and protests helped end his tenure there. Al-Attas, from another direction, argued that the crisis is not a lack of method but a loss of <i>adab</i>.",
 v:"Check the claim about the place of logic and theology relative to the Qur'an in the Dars-i Nizami against chapter 1."},

{n:"Al-Attas, The Concept of Education in Islam", t:"mod", home:"Islamic, Southeast Asia", d:"1980", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Syed Muhammad Naquib al-Attas", wk:"Syed_Muhammad_Naquib_al-Attas",
 mn:"mixed", es:"within", mo:"reverence", sc:"graded", au:"tradition", f:"f",
 read:"<i>The Concept of Education in Islam</i> (a short book from his 1977 address at the First World Conference on Muslim Education, Mecca); <i>Islam and Secularism</i> (1978), ch. 4", tr:"Original English", ta:["malaysia-falsafah-pendidikan-kebangsaan","Malaysia: Falsafah Pendidikan Kebangsaan"],
 w:"Al-Attas argues that the right Arabic word for education is <i>taʾdīb</i>, not <i>tarbiya</i> or <i>taʿlīm</i>. <i>Tarbiya</i>, rearing, can apply to plants and animals and to raising property. <i>Taʿlīm</i> is limited to cognitive instruction. <i>Taʾdīb</i>, the instilling of <i>adab</i>, contains both and adds the essential thing.",
 p:"A rational animal (<i>ḥayawān nāṭiq</i>), with <i>nāṭiq</i> meaning reason that articulates meaning; a being of soul and body who affirmed God before birth (Qur'an 7:172) and is prone to forget.",
 e:"A good person, not a good citizen. <i>Adab</i> is the recognition and acknowledgment of the proper place of things in the order of creation, leading to the recognition of God's place.",
 x:"Knowledge in its right order: individually obligatory knowledge first, then the communal sciences, arranged so that the soul knows where each belongs.",
 mv:"<i>Adab</i>: recognizing one's proper place before God and before those who know more, which makes the soul receptive.",
 scp:"Within a recognized hierarchy of knowledge and authority.",
 k:"Those who possess <i>adab</i>, within a recognized hierarchy of knowledge and authority. The crisis of Muslims, in his diagnosis, runs from loss of <i>adab</i> to confusion in knowledge to the rise of false leaders. He founded ISTAC in Kuala Lumpur in 1987 to embody the model.",
 wo:"The practical sciences are communal obligations, placed below the knowledge each person owes for himself.",
 mc:"<i>Adab</i>, recognizing the proper place of things: a judgment of rank and meaning, not of information.",
 o:"An egalitarian objection: knowing one's ‘proper place’ can sanctify a social hierarchy. Fazlur Rahman would answer that the crisis is historical and methodological, not a matter of recovering a lost ontology."},

{n:"Ngũgĩ, Decolonising the Mind", t:"mod", home:"African", d:"1986", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Ngũgĩ wa Thiong'o", wk:"Decolonising_the_Mind",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"honour", sc:"open", au:"tradition", f:"f",
 read:"Introduction and ch. 1 (‘The Language of African Literature’)", tr:"Original English, his farewell to English as his language of fiction",
 w:"Ngũgĩ recalls colonial schools in Kenya where a child caught speaking Gĩkũyũ was punished and made to wear a sign announcing his stupidity. English became the measure of intelligence, and the language of his education was no longer the language of his culture.",
 p:"A person formed within a language that carries a community's memory and way of seeing. Colonial schooling severs the child from that formation and alienates him from himself.",
 e:"Restoration of the relation between the person, the mother tongue and the community.",
 x:"Literature and learning in African languages.",
 mv:"Dignity. The colonial school relied on shame, hanging a sign on the child who spoke his mother tongue; the remedy is to restore the learner's pride in his language and people.",
 scp:"Every child, in a language of their own.",
 k:"The community and its language first, the school second.",
 wo:"Language carries a community's whole life, its work songs and labour included; schooling in a foreign tongue cuts the learner off from both.",
 mc:"A mother tongue, in which a community's memory lives.",
 o:"Chinua Achebe argued in ‘The African Writer and the English Language’ (1965) that English could be bent to carry African experience and was a tool too useful to abandon. The disagreement bears directly on teaching technical subjects in Urdu."},

{n:"MacIntyre, Tradition and the University", t:"mod", home:"European-American", d:"1981–1999", era:"Contemporary",
 by:"Alasdair MacIntyre", wk:"After_Virtue",
 mn:"neutral", es:"within", mo:"love", sc:"open", au:"tradition", f:"f",
 read:"<i>After Virtue</i> (1981), chs. 14–15; <i>Three Rival Versions of Moral Enquiry</i> (1990), ch. 10; ‘The Idea of an Educated Public’ (1987)", tr:"Original English",
 w:"MacIntyre argues that modern moral language is the fragmented remains of an older tradition of the virtues, and that education has lost the shared public it once served. In 1990 he proposed a university organized as a place of constrained disagreement between rival traditions of enquiry.",
 p:"A dependent rational animal, as his 1999 book is titled, and a storytelling animal whose life has a narrative unity, situated within traditions.",
 e:"The goods internal to practices, the good of a whole life understood as a quest, and the common good of a community.",
 x:"Practices, which are cooperative activities with their own standards of excellence; entry into a tradition, understood as a historically extended argument about the goods that constitute it.",
 mv:"A passage from external reward to internal goods. A child paid in sweets to play chess may come to want the goods of chess itself, and only then has he entered the practice (<i>After Virtue</i>, ch. 14).",
 scp:"Anyone who enters a practice and accepts its standards.",
 k:"The master of a practice, whose authority the apprentice must at first accept in order to learn its standards, though not forever. He has called teachers the forlorn hope of the culture of Western modernity.",
 wo:"Farming, fishing and architecture are among his examples of practices; work done only for wages loses the goods internal to it.",
 mc:"Goods internal to a practice, which can be had only by taking part in it.",
 o:"Liberal critics, Martha Nussbaum among them, argue that his traditions are less open to criticism than he claims and that his account cannot protect individuals from their communities.",
 v:"Confirm the source of ‘the forlorn hope of the culture of Western modernity’."}

